															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mrwrong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2039383); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2039383&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606207937" alt="MrWrong" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2039383)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/mrwrong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2039383); return false;" >MrWrong</a></span> <span class="odate time_1450065080 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Dec 2015 03:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2423229">
									

<p>Firstly, thanks to all reviewers such as minmin, BranHed, CumaeanSibyl, kola, Dr Solo, hihababa and etc.</p>
<p>Secondly, image credits go to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Image 1 by Zoharby of Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kh%C3%B6vsg%C3%B6l_Nuur">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kh%C3%B6vsg%C3%B6l_Nuur</a>) under CC BY-SA 3.0</li>
<li>Image 2 from Wikipedia (<a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kappa_water_imp_1836.jpg">https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kappa_water_imp_1836.jpg</a>) under Public Domain</li>
</ul>
<p>Thirdly, this article features the following</p>
<ul>
<li>SCP-1000 (Bigfoot) by thedeadlymoose</li>
</ul>
<p>Fourthly, read below.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><em>The kappa is more commonly found in Mongolia</em> – Severus Snape, <em>Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban</em></p>
<p>The weird implication is that the kappas' event might be tied to a fictional story written by humans. The reference to the Day of Flowers (from SCP-1000) hints that humanity's triumph over Bigfoot <em>might</em> or <em>might not</em> be the work of some kappas reading bedtime stories to their kin rather than anything glorious.</p>
<p>As for the final addendum, if we break down the wording, kappa in kanji literally means 河童 or &quot;river-child&quot;. A child fell into a river worked just as well, I suppose.</p>
<p>And of all things that must come true from fiction, why an obscure line that has no plot relevance in the book/series? God has weird ways of doing things, as they say.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

